WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:06.270 --> 00:00:11.129
Malcolm Grant: So may we then turn to the
next paper please, which is the 2015-16 Planning

00:00:11.129 --> 00:00:16.119
and Contracting Round. What I have in mind
her is perhaps to invite Paul to introduce

00:00:16.119 --> 00:00:21.190
the paper and then ask Barbara to talk about
some of the operations aspects of it, so Paul,

00:00:21.190 --> 00:00:22.800
may we kick of with you please.

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:26.630
Paul Baumann: You can indeed. I do not think
I can keep the wind metaphor going for long

00:00:26.630 --> 00:00:32.469
enough, so I will move on from it, if I may.
Of course, all members of the Board, whether

00:00:32.469 --> 00:00:38.570
executive or non executive, have been accompanying
us along the journey of the last few weeks

00:00:38.570 --> 00:00:43.200
and months of developing these plans, and
so in a way what we are going to talk about

00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:47.160
today is the culmination of a process that
you have all been very much steeped in as

00:00:47.160 --> 00:00:48.149
it has gone through.

00:00:48.149 --> 00:00:53.100
It has been a difficult planning and contracting
round this year, perhaps to no one's great

00:00:53.100 --> 00:00:59.040
surprise, and it has had a more extended timescale
than usual because of things like the complications

00:00:59.040 --> 00:01:04.309
that we had around the price setting, which
I will not rehearse again; the significant

00:01:04.309 --> 00:01:10.560
financial and operational challenges that
continue through from 2014/15 into 2015/16,

00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:16.139
and therefore the consequent need for real
rigour and due diligence in both the preparation

00:01:16.139 --> 00:01:21.289
of the plans by CCGs and our direct commissioning
teams and then the assurance of those plans

00:01:21.289 --> 00:01:26.240
by our regional and national leadership. It
really is important this year, more so than

00:01:26.240 --> 00:01:31.899
any previous year, that we get under the skin
of the plans and make sure that they are absolutely

00:01:31.899 --> 00:01:37.469
rock solid, given the winds that they will
have to withstand.

00:01:37.469 --> 00:01:42.020
That has been made possible by the fact that
in the second year it is much easier to compare

00:01:42.020 --> 00:01:47.159
and contrast trends than it is if you are
in your first year of set-up. For example,

00:01:47.159 --> 00:01:51.289
on activity, which we will talk a bit about
in this session, we do now have two years'

00:01:51.289 --> 00:01:55.259
worth of data, so you can start to see what
is happening year-on-year and over a longer

00:01:55.259 --> 00:01:59.709
period in our new commissioning structures.

00:01:59.709 --> 00:02:04.399
I am pleased to say - for all sorts of reasons,
but today because I think it is important

00:02:04.399 --> 00:02:09.179
that we get on with doing stuff - that the
process is now largely over; I will come back

00:02:09.179 --> 00:02:13.610
to the largely in a minute. We received a
full set of plans from all organisations,

00:02:13.610 --> 00:02:20.150
as you can see, on 14th May. Yesterday we
received updates to those plans, largely focused

00:02:20.150 --> 00:02:27.270
on more accurate projections on activity and
on constitutional standards. For most organisations

00:02:27.270 --> 00:02:32.060
we anticipate that will be the end of the
journey, we will have plans that we can sign

00:02:32.060 --> 00:02:35.840
off at this point, and then they can get on
with the really important work, not of planning

00:02:35.840 --> 00:02:38.980
but of delivering all of the stuff.

00:02:38.980 --> 00:02:43.470
We have set out in the paper the detailed
assurance that has been carried out around

00:02:43.470 --> 00:02:48.920
activity, which is frankly the underpinning
unit of the whole of our planning. I like

00:02:48.920 --> 00:02:52.980
to think the finance is kind of important.
Of course, we like to think that all of the

00:02:52.980 --> 00:02:56.480
targets that we are trying to work to and
all the other objectives are important. However,

00:02:56.480 --> 00:03:02.400
the fundamental planning unit of all of these
plans is activity. It is fair to say - and

00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:07.320
the paper puts it delicately - that our activity
planning in 2014/15 was not characterised

00:03:07.320 --> 00:03:13.680
by an enormous amount of realism. That is
probably a bit euphemistic as well. We have

00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:22.100
sought to strike a better balance in 2015/16
between ambition around efficiency, around

00:03:22.100 --> 00:03:30.430
inflecting the demand curve and making that
change that we need to make the service sustainable

00:03:30.430 --> 00:03:35.100
and patients have the right experience at
the right time in the right place on the one

00:03:35.100 --> 00:03:42.810
hand, bolstered by the Better Care Fund, which
we expect significant impact from, but on

00:03:42.810 --> 00:03:48.810
the other hand, the need to have really sound,
sustainable activity plans that are not going

00:03:48.810 --> 00:03:55.160
to leave us short of capacity to meet patient
demand as it takes place. That delicate balance

00:03:55.160 --> 00:03:59.280
is one of the reasons why it has taken us
a bit longer to get to a set of plans that

00:03:59.280 --> 00:04:05.130
we can put in front of you and say, 'These
are the right ones to sign off.'

00:04:05.130 --> 00:04:11.910
As my friend has already given you the information
on, we expect the final plans to reflect this

00:04:11.910 --> 00:04:17.660
in elective admissions growth of just under
3%, and non elective growth, including some

00:04:17.660 --> 00:04:23.430
benefit from the Better Care Fund, of just
over 2%. Both of those, when you look at it

00:04:23.430 --> 00:04:29.550
from the trend perspective, appear to be broadly
plausible, as I say, both in relation to the

00:04:29.550 --> 00:04:34.710
trends and the things that we know that CCGs
and direct commissioners are doing to modify

00:04:34.710 --> 00:04:38.180
those trends going forward.

00:04:38.180 --> 00:04:41.879
That is, if you like, the helicopter view
of it from looking at the trends nationally

00:04:41.879 --> 00:04:47.550
and trying to sense whether we have a set
of plans that reflects what you would expect.

00:04:47.550 --> 00:04:51.099
Barbara might want to say just a few words
about the rigour that has been put in bottom

00:04:51.099 --> 00:04:56.770
up to checking those plans, and indeed helping
colleagues around the country to prepare those

00:04:56.770 --> 00:05:02.419
plans, and secondly then perhaps some of the
consequential impacts on performance against

00:05:02.419 --> 00:05:06.759
constitutional standards etc., which flow
from that, before I come back to talk about

00:05:06.759 --> 00:05:11.289
the financial implications of all of that.

00:05:11.289 --> 00:05:16.610
Barbara Hakin: Thank you, Paul. Thank you,
Chairman. I am going to make no apologies

00:05:16.610 --> 00:05:24.270
for reiterating what has been said about the
delicate balance we have here in terms of

00:05:24.270 --> 00:05:28.719
getting the activity levels right so that
patients get what they need, because we as

00:05:28.719 --> 00:05:33.840
patients do not want to have to go to hospital
if we do not need to be in hospital, and yet

00:05:33.840 --> 00:05:40.960
when we do need to go to hospital we want
and need the right services to be there. Hence

00:05:40.960 --> 00:05:46.889
there is a tension between pushing forward
on all those initiatives that put in place

00:05:46.889 --> 00:05:51.919
the right services outside of hospital - in
our communities, in primary care, in people's

00:05:51.919 --> 00:05:56.520
homes - which help them to stay as well as
they can be and avoid the need for a hospital

00:05:56.520 --> 00:06:02.960
admission even if they are ill, and the balance
between ensuring that we are, as commissioners,

00:06:02.960 --> 00:06:08.059
putting enough certainty and security into
the hands of our providers so that they can

00:06:08.059 --> 00:06:12.949
plan the capacity that is needed for those
patients where it is inevitable that they

00:06:12.949 --> 00:06:14.979
need to go to hospital for their care.

00:06:14.979 --> 00:06:18.789
I really do want to pay tribute to all our
people on the front line, whether they are

00:06:18.789 --> 00:06:25.879
in CCGs or in the field force in NHS England,
for the meticulous amount of work that has

00:06:25.879 --> 00:06:31.789
been done to make sure that these activity
levels and the finance balances are right

00:06:31.789 --> 00:06:36.999
for individual communities. That is because,
sitting underneath a set of national numbers,

00:06:36.999 --> 00:06:41.089
there is huge variation; there are lots of
different things going on locally, and all

00:06:41.089 --> 00:06:46.360
the local circumstances need to be taken into
account to make sure that that capacity planning

00:06:46.360 --> 00:06:50.029
is right for the patients in that locality.

00:06:50.029 --> 00:06:54.930
We have had some delay, as Paul has alluded
to. We are perhaps slightly behind in terms

00:06:54.930 --> 00:06:59.789
of being certain about contracts. However,
I can report to the Board that the vast majority

00:06:59.789 --> 00:07:05.659
of contracts are on track to be signed, and
for the most part we have good agreement between

00:07:05.659 --> 00:07:13.240
commissioners and providers about the activity
levels that are needed. It is so important

00:07:13.240 --> 00:07:20.639
that providers are given that ability to plan.
We as commissioners have absolute responsibility

00:07:20.639 --> 00:07:26.319
to make sure that we get the best prediction
possible of the patients who are going to

00:07:26.319 --> 00:07:30.599
need to go to our secondary care providers
and ensure that those providers understand

00:07:30.599 --> 00:07:35.669
that that will be backed up by the finance
to pay for that activity. Then and only then

00:07:35.669 --> 00:07:41.339
can providers identify the capacity, whether
that is workforce or beds or whatever they

00:07:41.339 --> 00:07:46.689
need to put in place in order to be able to
respond, particularly around the NHS Constitution

00:07:46.689 --> 00:07:53.409
Standards and for them to put in place the
right capacity for patients to be treated

00:07:53.409 --> 00:07:55.809
in line with those Constitution Standards.

00:07:55.809 --> 00:08:01.180
Now, we also know that this is not easy, and
there are some places where it is difficult,

00:08:01.180 --> 00:08:06.180
in certain patches or for certain specialties,
to ensure that the right capacity is in place.

00:08:06.180 --> 00:08:12.289
Where that is the case, we - nationally, working
with TDA and Monitor, but much more important,

00:08:12.289 --> 00:08:18.520
locally, our front line staff - are working
with front line staff in provider organisations

00:08:18.520 --> 00:08:24.719
and in CCGs to identify whether there are
alternative sources of capacity that can be

00:08:24.719 --> 00:08:29.809
secured from other NHS organisations or from
the independent sector, but all of this of

00:08:29.809 --> 00:08:33.960
course depending on patient choice. In the
final analysis, particularly for elective

00:08:33.960 --> 00:08:38.760
care, patients choose where they need to go,
and the importance of this planning is to

00:08:38.760 --> 00:08:44.350
make the right predictions so that where patients
choose those providers, those providers can

00:08:44.350 --> 00:08:51.160
put in place the right capacity to treat those
patients in a timely way.

00:08:51.160 --> 00:08:57.750
I will not go into detail about all the work
that has been done across the piece, but just

00:08:57.750 --> 00:09:04.940
to say that for every single CCG, as part
of our role in assurance of CCGs, as part

00:09:04.940 --> 00:09:11.510
of NHS England's role for the full assurance
and oversight of the commissioning system,

00:09:11.510 --> 00:09:16.530
the people in our regional teams have worked
with every single CCG to look at the basis

00:09:16.530 --> 00:09:22.530
on which they plan. This starts, as Paul rightly
pointed out, with trends. What has been happening

00:09:22.530 --> 00:09:28.580
in your locality? What is the pattern with
emergency admissions? What is the pattern

00:09:28.580 --> 00:09:34.210
with GP referrals? It then builds on the back
of those trends to understand what initiatives

00:09:34.210 --> 00:09:38.900
might have taken place and what other factors
might mean that different places need very

00:09:38.900 --> 00:09:46.840
different levels of activity. Particularly
for elective activity, what we know is that

00:09:46.840 --> 00:09:51.370
certain places have built up a bigger backlog
of patients who are waiting for treatment

00:09:51.370 --> 00:09:57.140
than others. Therefore, inevitably, the level
of elective activity that they need to commit

00:09:57.140 --> 00:09:59.980
the finances to for next year is much greater.

00:09:59.980 --> 00:10:05.490
Similarly, we know that in some places CCGs
and providers have agreed a coding change.

00:10:05.490 --> 00:10:09.970
I think this is important for it to be focused
on what matters for patients, but there is

00:10:09.970 --> 00:10:15.700
a lot of technical detail sitting behind that,
and some of the increases in some places that

00:10:15.700 --> 00:10:20.710
we see, which would suggest that you were
getting very high levels of increase in elective

00:10:20.710 --> 00:10:25.090
activity, are simply as a result of a coding
change that has been agreed locally, something

00:10:25.090 --> 00:10:30.490
that might have moved from an outpatient to
an inpatient procedure or differentially coded.

00:10:30.490 --> 00:10:35.630
It is clear that we have to clear that out
to understand what the real patient need is,

00:10:35.630 --> 00:10:39.530
and not be buying activity on the basis of
something that was simply a coding change

00:10:39.530 --> 00:10:41.940
in the preceding year.

00:10:41.940 --> 00:10:49.450
The final thing that our regional teams have
done with CCGs is to look at those schemes

00:10:49.450 --> 00:10:53.800
that I talked about earlier that help to avoid
hospital admission where it is really not

00:10:53.800 --> 00:10:59.700
in the best interest of the patient. Some
of those are schemes that we have been undertaking

00:10:59.700 --> 00:11:04.270
for a number of years in the NHS and which
are traditionally called QIPP schemes; it

00:11:04.270 --> 00:11:08.960
is about quality and improving productivity.
However, many of the big schemes this year,

00:11:08.960 --> 00:11:13.500
as Paul rightly pointed out, are within the
Better Care Fund. We have a lot of ambition

00:11:13.500 --> 00:11:18.310
within the Better Care Fund working across
health and social care, working with local

00:11:18.310 --> 00:11:22.950
authorities to ensure that the right services
are in place for patients, which we hope will

00:11:22.950 --> 00:11:27.770
have a very significant impact, particularly
on emergency admissions.

00:11:27.770 --> 00:11:32.940
It was essential to support CCGs and their
local authority partners and individual patches

00:11:32.940 --> 00:11:38.810
in really assessing how far down the line
those schemes were in delivering from day

00:11:38.810 --> 00:11:44.790
one, and, again, a rigorous process for every
individual patch about all their individual

00:11:44.790 --> 00:11:50.120
schemes and trying to make sure that we understood
when the impact would be felt. Is this scheme

00:11:50.120 --> 00:11:54.970
something that is already fully resolved,
good clinical and stakeholder buy in, all

00:11:54.970 --> 00:11:59.080
the systems and processes are in place and
is expected to hit the ground running on day

00:11:59.080 --> 00:12:04.570
one and, therefore, have a full year impact?
Or are some schemes really brilliant ideas

00:12:04.570 --> 00:12:08.660
that we expect to come to fruition but will
take more time in the year, and therefore

00:12:08.660 --> 00:12:13.620
we cannot count the full reduction in activity
that we will expect to see in the fullness

00:12:13.620 --> 00:12:14.150
of time?

00:12:14.150 --> 00:12:20.840
Those are the three things that the regional
teams have done, working with our CCGs, to

00:12:20.840 --> 00:12:24.920
make sure that the CCG plans cover the right
amount of activity, because of course for

00:12:24.920 --> 00:12:30.870
the most part CCGs commission the bulk of
elective and urgent care activity, but also

00:12:30.870 --> 00:12:36.060
undertaking exactly the same process for NHS
England's direct commissioning, whether it

00:12:36.060 --> 00:12:39.550
is specialised or some of the other elements
of direct commissioning that we do.

00:12:39.550 --> 00:12:46.890
There are two other points that I want to
draw to the Board's attention. Firstly, in

00:12:46.890 --> 00:12:56.510
the past, we have dispensed money for winter
and resilience as the year has gone on. This

00:12:56.510 --> 00:13:02.490
year, the government and we have been very
clear that operational resilience money for

00:13:02.490 --> 00:13:07.370
winter planning is in baselines, and we have
been able to work with front line organisations

00:13:07.370 --> 00:13:12.070
to make sure that they have good resilience
plans to take them through the winter, when

00:13:12.070 --> 00:13:19.260
we know we will have that surge in activity.
There will be further work to do, however,

00:13:19.260 --> 00:13:23.760
on those resilience plans. We are a lot further
ahead than we normally are, but we will continue

00:13:23.760 --> 00:13:29.970
to monitor and oversee those resilience plans
as we go through the summer months. This is

00:13:29.970 --> 00:13:35.570
to make sure that throughout the year we have
secured the right capacity with our providers,

00:13:35.570 --> 00:13:40.160
the activity is following the right levels,
making adjustments where that is not happening,

00:13:40.160 --> 00:13:46.440
in order to make sure that providers can then
support us in meeting the Constitutional Standards

00:13:46.440 --> 00:13:48.140
in particular.

00:13:48.140 --> 00:13:52.830
The final point I would like to make is it
is inevitable that this planning round focuses

00:13:52.830 --> 00:14:01.310
very heavily on activity and finance for acute
activity, for physical healthcare needs. I

00:14:01.310 --> 00:14:07.850
would not want the Board to underestimate
that, as well as that there is a whole host

00:14:07.850 --> 00:14:13.680
of other sets of plans for other things which
we want to deliver for patients. I would particularly

00:14:13.680 --> 00:14:19.690
say that that is the case in terms of mental
health. The detail is there for any Board

00:14:19.690 --> 00:14:25.870
members who wish to see it, but again, a huge
amount of work has gone on with mental health.

00:14:25.870 --> 00:14:32.180
Firstly, in terms of parity of esteem, CCGs
and specialised commissioning, we are investing

00:14:32.180 --> 00:14:41.050
equitably in mental health, ensuring that
the standards that have been set for mental

00:14:41.050 --> 00:14:45.290
health, IAPT, dementia, early intervention
and psychosis, and the new mental health access

00:14:45.290 --> 00:14:47.030
targets will be delivered.

00:14:47.030 --> 00:14:52.490
There are other things as well as those, but
these two big critical parcels of activity

00:14:52.490 --> 00:14:56.540
and then making sure that we are prepared
for the mental health initiatives that we

00:14:56.540 --> 00:15:02.830
want to see and the standards we want to see
delivered.

00:15:02.830 --> 00:15:08.360
Paul Baumann: Very briefly, just to the humble
art of the finance director at the end, on

00:15:08.360 --> 00:15:13.710
the financial front the plans have been constructed
essentially on some key assumptions, which

00:15:13.710 --> 00:15:17.710
we need to eyeball just once again.

00:15:17.710 --> 00:15:23.000
The first of those is we have allocated the
£101 billion recurrently to the system that

00:15:23.000 --> 00:15:27.000
we spent a lot of time in December talking
about. That is the first component.

00:15:27.000 --> 00:15:32.720
The second component is the £579 million
of prior-year surplus drawdown, which I referred

00:15:32.720 --> 00:15:36.360
to in the 2014/15 section.

00:15:36.360 --> 00:15:42.850
The third is the £150 million for the Enhanced
Tariff Option that we put in place, central

00:15:42.850 --> 00:15:44.940
government funding for that.

00:15:44.940 --> 00:15:49.970
The fourth bit, which needs to be added to
the current plans, is the mental health money,

00:15:49.970 --> 00:15:56.610
the total budget of 250 per annum that we
will invest over the next four or five years

00:15:56.610 --> 00:15:58.380
behind all of that.

00:15:58.380 --> 00:16:02.000
Those are really important building blocks
that we have worked into the plans that I

00:16:02.000 --> 00:16:03.430
am about to talk about.

00:16:03.430 --> 00:16:08.840
The plans obviously reflect the conscious
decisions we took in December to skew our

00:16:08.840 --> 00:16:14.860
allocations to CCGs to try to address more
quickly the underfunding issues which Simon

00:16:14.860 --> 00:16:20.760
referred to earlier in the meeting. Those
have now been worked through, which has created

00:16:20.760 --> 00:16:27.680
some more meaningful plans, frankly, for some
of the CCGs that were under funded to date.

00:16:27.680 --> 00:16:33.800
I am pleased to say that we now do have a
set of agreed financial plans which reach

00:16:33.800 --> 00:16:38.650
balance against those building blocks that
I have just talked about both for CCGs and

00:16:38.650 --> 00:16:44.340
for the direct commissioning teams. With a
few minor exceptions, I think we will be able

00:16:44.340 --> 00:16:50.650
to sign off individual plans. The minor exceptions
are predominantly around the mental health

00:16:50.650 --> 00:16:56.670
parity of esteem that Barbara mentioned before.
Those plans that we do sign off will have

00:16:56.670 --> 00:17:01.120
clear articulations, in particular where they
are using prior-year surpluses, as to what

00:17:01.120 --> 00:17:05.429
the investment is that they are putting into
that. Part of our reassurance that we had

00:17:05.429 --> 00:17:10.189
in the process was to look carefully at those
plans and into those who had above average

00:17:10.189 --> 00:17:15.100
growth from the allocations, to check that
that money was being invested in the right

00:17:15.100 --> 00:17:20.120
way behind the right strategic priorities.
I can give the Board assurance that that has

00:17:20.120 --> 00:17:23.030
taken place and will have its impact.

00:17:23.030 --> 00:17:28.690
At the risk of creating the screaming headlines
of tomorrow, I just need to draw your attention

00:17:28.690 --> 00:17:32.790
before I close to the risk profile that sits
around those plans. It is all very well to

00:17:32.790 --> 00:17:37.000
have balanced plans that we have put together;
we need to look consciously, as we do at this

00:17:37.000 --> 00:17:41.590
time of year and indeed throughout the year,
at the ups and downs there are against those

00:17:41.590 --> 00:17:42.700
plans.

00:17:42.700 --> 00:17:48.640
The 14th May submissions contained the usual
bottom up evaluation of those risks and available

00:17:48.640 --> 00:17:54.630
mitigations alongside the balanced main plan
numbers. This indicated a net risk of about

00:17:54.630 --> 00:18:01.400
£160 million, net of mitigations that we
have currently identified. That is a serious

00:18:01.400 --> 00:18:06.309
issue. It is an issue to which we need to
put real attention as we go into the year.

00:18:06.309 --> 00:18:11.840
I would remind you, though, it is 0.2% of
our total allocation, and therefore not the

00:18:11.840 --> 00:18:14.490
biggest issue, I think, for us to attack.

00:18:14.490 --> 00:18:21.160
We have a balanced approach, therefore, to
manage for the year against the risks that

00:18:21.160 --> 00:18:24.920
we can see there, and indeed those that are
not, if you like, bottom up risks - things

00:18:24.920 --> 00:18:30.300
like the Cancer Drugs Fund and the growth
in new drugs, which I mentioned earlier. This

00:18:30.300 --> 00:18:36.020
may well have moved on in the submissions
that we had yesterday. Even with our approach

00:18:36.020 --> 00:18:43.190
to financial management, we cannot turn overnight
230 plans into new statistics. It will have

00:18:43.190 --> 00:18:47.809
moved on, but I suspect that the risk profile
will still be there in large measure for us

00:18:47.809 --> 00:18:50.770
to deal with as we go forward.

00:18:50.770 --> 00:18:55.040
That makes it all the more important - and
again, this is referred to in the paper - that

00:18:55.040 --> 00:18:59.010
we are upping our game in the interventions
that Ed was talking about in terms of 2014/15.

00:18:59.010 --> 00:19:04.010
Therefore, we have agreed with the Investment
Committee a set of very concrete measures

00:19:04.010 --> 00:19:10.260
to be better at earlier detection, or indeed
ideally prevention, of individual organisations

00:19:10.260 --> 00:19:16.559
going off beam as the year goes by. We had
a few of those in 2014/15. We expect to detect

00:19:16.559 --> 00:19:21.520
them earlier and then intervene earlier and
in a more joined-up fashion of any of those

00:19:21.520 --> 00:19:25.020
that occur during the course of the year.
We will doubtless have reason to come back

00:19:25.020 --> 00:19:29.760
to that as the year goes by, but again I want
to reassure you that we have taken the learnings

00:19:29.760 --> 00:19:35.770
from 2014/15 and the specific issues that
we encountered in specific places in 2014/15

00:19:35.770 --> 00:19:41.750
and translated that into a much better way,
I think, of avoiding those sorts of things

00:19:41.750 --> 00:19:46.520
hitting us during 2015/16, or where they do,
at dealing with them quickly and resolutely.

00:19:46.520 --> 00:19:49.700
That is all I wanted to say about 2015/16.

00:19:49.700 --> 00:19:54.910
Malcolm Grant: Paul and Barbara, thank you
very much. On that very last point, I have

00:19:54.910 --> 00:19:58.750
a question more for Barbara. We have done
an enormous amount with this commissioning

00:19:58.750 --> 00:20:06.910
round. We can see the alignment between commissioning
and provision, we think, to be much closer

00:20:06.910 --> 00:20:11.340
and much more realistic. However, it has taken
an enormous amount of work on the part of

00:20:11.340 --> 00:20:20.050
your ops people. To what extent have your
experiences this year brought about, with

00:20:20.050 --> 00:20:26.590
a maturing CCG sector, an upping of game and
an ability next year to operate of course

00:20:26.590 --> 00:20:31.790
with the scrutiny that we need to give them,
but without the detailed support and involvement

00:20:31.790 --> 00:20:34.800
that your team have had to invest in the current
round?

00:20:34.800 --> 00:20:39.800
Barbara Hakin: To be honest, it is a good
point, Chairman, and it is an issue for all

00:20:39.800 --> 00:20:47.380
of us. It is an issue for us in NHS England
and the CCGs, the capacity and capability

00:20:47.380 --> 00:20:54.770
to undertake this detailed work. I think that
everyone would say that that capability is

00:20:54.770 --> 00:21:01.780
something that we lost not only on the commission
side, but I think the provider sector would

00:21:01.780 --> 00:21:09.480
stand by us and say that some of the capacity
and capability that they had to do, comprehensive

00:21:09.480 --> 00:21:15.280
capacity planning, activity understanding
during the last few years, has not been as

00:21:15.280 --> 00:21:20.880
strong in the NHS. Of course, as Paul alluded
to, the change in the organisational shape

00:21:20.880 --> 00:21:28.540
that we had left us with a reduction in the
information ability and the comprehensiveness

00:21:28.540 --> 00:21:33.410
and the complexity of the information that
we have. Therefore, this year has been a big

00:21:33.410 --> 00:21:39.050
learning curve for everyone, our teams, provider
teams and CCGs.

00:21:39.050 --> 00:21:46.270
I think that everyone has come out of it recognising
that working together is the key. In the final

00:21:46.270 --> 00:21:51.059
analysis, there will always be risks that
commissioners and providers have to take.

00:21:51.059 --> 00:21:54.929
Commissioners have to determine how much activity
they think is right, providers have to determine

00:21:54.929 --> 00:21:59.059
the capacity that they put in place for the
activity that is expected, and each organisation

00:21:59.059 --> 00:22:06.920
has to balance its risk. However, if we pool
our expertise as well as pooling the way we

00:22:06.920 --> 00:22:08.140
do business, then that is a big help.

00:22:08.140 --> 00:22:12.750
Malcolm Grant: How will it look next year
by comparison to this?

00:22:12.750 --> 00:22:19.260
Barbara Hakin: I think it will always be difficult,
but we will have CCGs that have learnt from

00:22:19.260 --> 00:22:26.580
this experience, I hope. As an organisation,
we have always guided our front line teams

00:22:26.580 --> 00:22:31.210
that this should always be done in a supportive
and helpful way, and I know that they have

00:22:31.210 --> 00:22:36.950
all attempted to do that. I think CCGs will
have learnt very significantly from that.

00:22:36.950 --> 00:22:42.150
However, we will also benefit from the new
CCG assurance process that we are going through

00:22:42.150 --> 00:22:47.130
at the moment. We are out consulting with
stakeholders. It is a more comprehensive process.

00:22:47.130 --> 00:22:54.130
It should be able much more quickly to identify
which organisations we can leave alone to

00:22:54.130 --> 00:22:59.400
do their business because they have the capacity
and capability to do it, and which ones we

00:22:59.400 --> 00:23:03.620
really need to focus on and where we might
have governance issues that we should pick

00:23:03.620 --> 00:23:04.280
up sooner.

00:23:04.280 --> 00:23:10.240
Just as a final point on that: this has not
only been a difficult time for CCGs and NHS

00:23:10.240 --> 00:23:16.250
England, but CSUs are vital in this. The business
intelligence and the capacity and capability

00:23:16.250 --> 00:23:22.780
in CSUs is massive. There are 10,000 staff
out there in CSUs for whom this is bread and

00:23:22.780 --> 00:23:29.240
butter business, and they have gone through
a lot of turmoil and change. That is all beginning

00:23:29.240 --> 00:23:32.950
to settle down, and therefore they should
be in a better position next year to support

00:23:32.950 --> 00:23:35.470
CCGs and NHS England through this process.

00:23:35.470 --> 00:23:43.090
Malcolm Grant: Okay, thank you very much.
I invite Ed to come and anyone else who wishes

00:23:43.090 --> 00:23:43.220
to follow up.

00:23:43.220 --> 00:23:48.130
Ed Smith: I would just say that this is still
a relatively young system that is in an established

00:23:48.130 --> 00:23:56.730
world, and so we should not be surprised that
this is still a growing system.

00:23:56.730 --> 00:24:03.799
In response to Malcolm's question, I have
the opportunity to meet 60-70% of the lay

00:24:03.799 --> 00:24:11.820
CCG chairs twice a year with Paul and other
colleagues, and even though we will be on

00:24:11.820 --> 00:24:18.809
top of what they did last year in the coming
year, they are being asked to do new things,

00:24:18.809 --> 00:24:24.220
and co commissioning and other things are
going to be new ambitions for the CCGs. Therefore,

00:24:24.220 --> 00:24:29.710
we will always I think be having to have a
degree of oversight as the system matures,

00:24:29.710 --> 00:24:35.200
and I would not say any time fast, Malcolm,
in response to your question of will it be

00:24:35.200 --> 00:24:43.580
very different in terms of the interaction
of the regional teams and the CCGs. If you

00:24:43.580 --> 00:24:48.940
look at a CCG, they are quite thinly stretched
in terms of their oversight, governance capability.

00:24:48.940 --> 00:24:58.910
I had a number of people saying, 'We need
a bit more lay member support and involvement

00:24:58.910 --> 00:25:01.850
here because we are stretched.' I just think
we need to watch that stuff.

00:25:01.850 --> 00:25:07.809
Barbara Hakin: If I can just add on the back
of that, it is not only a new system, these

00:25:07.809 --> 00:25:12.540
are very challenging times when I look back
to when I was a PCT chief executive and the

00:25:12.540 --> 00:25:19.179
levels of growth that were coming into the
NHS on which we could invest in new initiatives

00:25:19.179 --> 00:25:24.780
to keep people out of hospital. Thus not only
do we have an immature system, but we must

00:25:24.780 --> 00:25:31.160
not underestimate the difficulty in which
CCGs, providers and our people are working

00:25:31.160 --> 00:25:33.860
out there because of the economic challenge
that we are facing.

00:25:33.860 --> 00:25:38.880
Malcolm Grant: Under those circumstances,
getting a realistic alignment between commissioning

00:25:38.880 --> 00:25:42.660
and provision is particularly challenging.

00:25:42.660 --> 00:25:49.000
John Burn: My question involves deputy and
specialist commissioning, and as a specialist

00:25:49.000 --> 00:25:54.390
in rare disease, therefore especially interested
in it. It is a question on the balance between

00:25:54.390 --> 00:26:02.809
direct commissioning for specialised disorders
and rare disorders, and what goes into CCGs.

00:26:02.809 --> 00:26:07.309
To what extent is that an accidental balance
as opposed to a strategic one, and what is

00:26:07.309 --> 00:26:14.950
our direction of travel in terms of the proportion
of our budget that we disperse through specialised

00:26:14.950 --> 00:26:21.140
commissioning? Is that something that we need
to be thoughtful about as a Board as more

00:26:21.140 --> 00:26:25.090
pressures come in on the specialised front?

00:26:25.090 --> 00:26:33.280
Simon Stevens: That is a difficult question.

00:26:33.280 --> 00:26:37.510
John Burn: I am sorry. It is the one that
is in my head.

00:26:37.510 --> 00:26:41.020
Simon Stevens: As you know, we have previously
talked about wanting to make sure that, wherever

00:26:41.020 --> 00:26:46.570
possible, we have place based budgets, even
if not hard budgets, then at least the ability

00:26:46.570 --> 00:26:52.640
to make sensible decisions for whole populations
about the balance of services. Equally, however,

00:26:52.640 --> 00:26:58.309
there were particular reasons why a separate
national specialist commissioning pot was

00:26:58.309 --> 00:27:04.720
established with responsibility given to NHS
England. It is for the Department of Health

00:27:04.720 --> 00:27:10.980
to decide which services fall within which
category. Therefore, as we think about 2016/17,

00:27:10.980 --> 00:27:17.010
we will be bringing proposals before you later
in the year as to whether or not we need to

00:27:17.010 --> 00:27:24.660
make any further adjustments for 2016/17.
For 2015/16 though, we have clarity, we have

00:27:24.660 --> 00:27:31.010
mechanisms for CCGs to influence the decisions
that we are required to make that cover their

00:27:31.010 --> 00:27:36.140
populations, and that is the basis on which
we are proceeding with the round that Paul

00:27:36.140 --> 00:27:36.920
has just described.

00:27:41.120 --> 00:27:45.799
Ciaran Devane: The realism comment I think
is well made. Can I just bring us back to

00:27:45.799 --> 00:27:50.830
the conversation we had about the Isle of
Wight? What we have just been talking about

00:27:50.830 --> 00:27:57.429
is linking the money to the activity, which
is a major step forward, but on the concept

00:27:57.429 --> 00:28:03.860
of one island pound, one of the asks of us
was outcomes based commissioning. And we have

00:28:03.860 --> 00:28:07.470
made a step in the direction, but this is
not outcomes based commissioning. I just want

00:28:07.470 --> 00:28:13.730
to mark that this must be part of a journey,
and when David's committee gets its teeth

00:28:13.730 --> 00:28:19.470
into how do we shift into that, there will
be more iterations. However, if we were to

00:28:19.470 --> 00:28:23.120
be unrealistic about activity, then you can
never get to the outcomes based commissioning

00:28:23.120 --> 00:28:28.770
as well. It is a step in the direction, fantastic,
but let us recognise it is a step in a direction.

00:28:28.770 --> 00:28:37.799
Margaret Casely Hayford: My questions are
about commissioning as an impetus for the

00:28:37.799 --> 00:28:45.820
right behaviour. For instance, you mentioned
the importance of investing in the right strategic

00:28:45.820 --> 00:28:54.440
priorities, and from that perspective for
example mental health being an important investment.

00:28:54.440 --> 00:29:02.309
I just want to make sure that we are comfortable
that, for example, invisible prods are being

00:29:02.309 --> 00:29:08.370
used when it comes to the listening mechanisms,
so that investment in things like, for example,

00:29:08.370 --> 00:29:16.590
NHS Citizen or the listening mechanisms are
sufficiently provided for. That is one behavioural

00:29:16.590 --> 00:29:23.380
key piece of commissioning machinery that
we need to be on top of, and another is almost

00:29:23.380 --> 00:29:30.840
the flip side of building in capacity. It
is to make sure that we are fostering the

00:29:30.840 --> 00:29:36.540
right behaviour in the providers, because
of course if you are looking at building in

00:29:36.540 --> 00:29:50.120
capacity there is also risk that CCGs face
a risk in being able to control the expenditure.

00:29:50.120 --> 00:29:58.210
How are we making sure that contractually
there is the right support for them to be

00:29:58.210 --> 00:30:07.260
able to foster the right provider behaviour,
I suppose is what I am asking.

00:30:07.260 --> 00:30:14.280
Barbara Hakin: The first point is a really
valid one and draws on the last question,

00:30:14.280 --> 00:30:22.610
because CCGs do work with their local populations
and try their best to do as much citizen and

00:30:22.610 --> 00:30:29.630
public and patient engagement as they can.
However, Tim and his team lend support to

00:30:29.630 --> 00:30:33.870
strengthen what is in CCGs, and we know that
it is not as good as it could be in some places.

00:30:33.870 --> 00:30:37.640
What is probably critical for patients and
the public, though, is that that is joined

00:30:37.640 --> 00:30:42.780
up with the NHS England stakeholder citizen
support for the services that we commission,

00:30:42.780 --> 00:30:47.650
and as we move down the co commissioning journey
and back to place based commissioning we ought

00:30:47.650 --> 00:30:55.549
to be able to, between us, strengthen the
focus on that individual place and do a better

00:30:55.549 --> 00:31:01.309
joined up effort. Again, we are dividing precious
resource if lots of different people are trying

00:31:01.309 --> 00:31:06.520
to engage with citizens and the public and
not making it easy for them.

00:31:06.520 --> 00:31:15.450
It is difficult for CCGs in terms of making
sure that the activity they plan is what ensues,

00:31:15.450 --> 00:31:19.260
and that we really do put the systems and
processes in place that will keep people away

00:31:19.260 --> 00:31:25.330
from hospital if they do not need it. However,
in the final analysis, we set CCGs up as membership

00:31:25.330 --> 00:31:30.289
organisations of GP practices, and we know
that GP practices and primary care is absolutely

00:31:30.289 --> 00:31:34.950
key and central to making sure that those
GP practices and the community services are

00:31:34.950 --> 00:31:43.970
in place for patients. We must not forget
that CCGs, because of their very nature, are

00:31:43.970 --> 00:31:49.809
best placed to influence the clinical behaviour,
and of course those GPs are the providers

00:31:49.809 --> 00:31:52.400
of many of those services as well.

00:31:52.400 --> 00:31:57.510
I think people do worry about provider behaviour
in terms of capture of patients, and I wonder

00:31:57.510 --> 00:32:02.480
if that is what you were alluding to, but
my experience of providers is that they really

00:32:02.480 --> 00:32:06.440
want to do the very best for patients. They
really do not want patients in hospitals who

00:32:06.440 --> 00:32:13.960
do not need to be there. They are really trying
to work hard with their CCGs and their local

00:32:13.960 --> 00:32:23.179
authorities to make sure that they get 
that right.

00:32:23.179 --> 00:32:30.160
Tim Kelsey: I heard loud and clear from both
Barbara and Paul that there is no more money

00:32:30.160 --> 00:32:38.240
for winter, and I think it behoves us to really
understand that. What that means, I think,

00:32:38.240 --> 00:32:43.039
is that it is not just about the aggregate
capacity planning during the year, but it

00:32:43.039 --> 00:32:49.559
is also about the scheduling of that capacity
and in particular the local system resilience

00:32:49.559 --> 00:32:56.730
work that needs to be done. Therefore, I guess
my question is: can the Board be assured particularly,

00:32:56.730 --> 00:33:02.169
Barbara, from the work that you and your teams
have done, that the capacity will be in place

00:33:02.169 --> 00:33:07.820
not just in aggregate, but at the right time?
Secondly, that the appropriate systems resilience

00:33:07.820 --> 00:33:13.360
work has been done and will continue to be
done? Because clearly the pressures of last

00:33:13.360 --> 00:33:21.500
year were partly as a result of mismatch between
expectation and capacity, and also there was

00:33:21.500 --> 00:33:29.799
money last year that will not be there this
year. I am hoping the answer to that is yes.

00:33:29.799 --> 00:33:34.679
Barbara Hakin: The system resilience groups
were set up by NHS England but are not a part

00:33:34.679 --> 00:33:39.190
of NHS England - remember that our formal
accountability line is to ensure that CCGs

00:33:39.190 --> 00:33:45.530
commission the right activity. We support
system resilience groups to look at all the

00:33:45.530 --> 00:33:49.419
capacity that is needed across the system
and all the initiatives that will help keep

00:33:49.419 --> 00:33:54.890
patients out of hospital. Some of those initiatives
fall to either NHS England as the primary

00:33:54.890 --> 00:34:03.669
commissioner through GP practices to provide,
but most of those initiatives then fall - once

00:34:03.669 --> 00:34:08.679
the pattern and the plan is put in place - to
providers to put the right capacity in place

00:34:08.679 --> 00:34:13.109
to meet that need, whether those are providers
in primary care, community services or secondary

00:34:13.109 --> 00:34:19.260
care. This is why it is so absolutely critical
that people on the ground work locally, commissioners

00:34:19.260 --> 00:34:24.840
and providers, and we work locally with TDA
and Monitor. Our colleagues in TDA and Monitor

00:34:24.840 --> 00:34:29.220
also have an enormous role to play, as we
do, in making sure that that capacity is in

00:34:29.220 --> 00:34:33.590
place and that, as a tripartite working with
other arms length bodies as well, we support

00:34:33.590 --> 00:34:37.879
those system resilience groups to work across
the whole community. However, I think it would

00:34:37.879 --> 00:34:44.040
be wrong to say that we, in NHS England, can
give assurance that the capacity is then being

00:34:44.040 --> 00:34:48.859
put in place through providers, because they
are still doing their plans, we are still

00:34:48.859 --> 00:34:52.080
triangulating, Monitor and TDA and still working
with them towards that.

00:34:52.080 --> 00:34:56.320
Tim Kelsey: Therefore, Simon, at some stage,
through your conversations with the other

00:34:56.320 --> 00:35:01.090
arms length bodies and the work that they
are doing, will we be able to get assurance

00:35:01.090 --> 00:35:07.260
that the system in aggregate has better capacity
planning for winter and for the right times?

00:35:07.260 --> 00:35:14.550
Simon Stevens: We certainly want that, but
as Ciaran in a sense implied, we are buying

00:35:14.550 --> 00:35:21.580
volume and are moving towards buying outcomes;
we are not buying beds and capacity. Therefore

00:35:21.580 --> 00:35:26.520
our responsibility, with our partners, is
to make sure that we try to size the funding

00:35:26.520 --> 00:35:31.640
requirement, the likely size of the emergency
admissions that are going to need to be treated,

00:35:31.640 --> 00:35:36.380
and then providers individually and collectively
are obviously working to ensure that there

00:35:36.380 --> 00:35:42.690
is appropriate resilience in the system to
deal with whatever next winter will bring.

00:35:42.690 --> 00:35:46.270
That is the division of labour that exists
in the system.

00:35:46.270 --> 00:35:52.910
Malcolm Grant: Thank you very much. I think
we have probably given that a good run. Forecasting

00:35:52.910 --> 00:36:00.050
is always difficult, is it not, especially
for the future? What we are doing here is

00:36:00.050 --> 00:36:05.470
trying to make sure we do a better fist of
it than last year, that we get better alignment

00:36:05.470 --> 00:36:10.890
between commissioners' plans and providers'
plans. I think Simon's last point, building

00:36:10.890 --> 00:36:17.880
on Ciaran's comment, that we are buying outcomes
and not physical capacity is critically important,

00:36:17.880 --> 00:36:23.550
but there are many other shifting stands in
amongst this that we need to be able to bring

00:36:23.550 --> 00:36:32.460
to the Board's attention on a regular basis,
changes in the real world as the year progresses.

00:36:32.460 --> 00:36:36.300
Thank you very much for the paper. The Board
takes the assurance that you have been able

00:36:36.300 --> 00:36:36.720
to give us.

